{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC6's CELL IS WRITTEN — Gerrit 1342. The existing idempotence cell does NOT cover it.\n\nI checked before writing rather than assuming, and the gap is real: `test_a_repeat_pass_over_a_converged_pair_plans_nothing` converges a **title edit on a plain task**. Idempotence is a property of the WRITER and the READER agreeing about one field's value, so a summary proving quiet says nothing about a parent — the epic parent is written outbound as the Epic Link custom field and read back inbound by `get_parent_map`'s Epic Link branch, the two halves this ticket built. If they disagree, every pass re-emits the same attach and the store churns forever.\n\n`test_a_repeat_pass_over_a_converged_epic_parent_plans_nothing` converges an epic-parent pair through a real pass, confirms the attach landed **by raw REST**, then **waits on `get_parent_map`** — the production read, not a direct GET — before re-planning. That wait is what makes a subsequent non-empty plan attributable to churn rather than index lag; without it the cell cannot tell the two apart.\n\nKept as a SEPARATE cell from the emit one, per this module's own recorded lesson: bundling idempotence into round-trip cells is what buried the real signal under thirteen false reds on J11's first run.\n\nAC6 stays UNTICKED until it runs green. Round-2 (30870845838) predates it."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785810500962683001,"uuid":"1580accd-445b-4d21-a8c6-f87745b5799c"}